  <!-- =========================================================================================
       (2) THE FRAME-FIRST FAMILY — a frame setup at offset 0, with the register saves (if any)
       AFTER it. 239 of WAR2's 1556 framed functions, and the ONLY shape modern Open Watcom emits
       under `-of+`.

       THIS IS x86gcc_patterns.xml's OWN FRAME-FIRST SET, COMPLETED. `push ebp; mov ebp,esp` is
       not a gcc idiom, so Ghidra's patterns for it carry over unchanged — but this file
       originally took only the two that require a following `sub esp`, and Ghidra ships six.
       The four that were left behind are exactly the BARE frame-first prologue, i.e. the shape
       with no `sub esp` at all:

           x86gcc #3  0x5589e5..83ec              frame, 1 filler byte, sub esp
           x86gcc #4  0x5589e5....83ec            frame, 2 filler bytes, sub esp
           x86gcc #5  0x5589e5 01010... 01010...  frame, then two register saves
           x86gcc #6  0x5589e58b 01...101         frame, then `mov r32,[ebp+disp8]`

       and that omission is measurable: per the warcraft2-re census **81% of framed WAR2
       functions have no `sub esp`** (save-first 891 without / 426 with, frame-first 187 / 52), so
       a set that demands one cannot see most of them. Bare `55 89 e5` is only 24 bits of check
       and Ghidra never states it alone anywhere in its x86 set — every one of its frame-first
       patterns adds discriminating bytes, or is paired with the filler that ends the previous
       function (family 4 below). This file does the same: nothing here is a naked `0x5589e5`.

       #5 IS TIGHTENED WITH WATCOM'S SAVE ORDER, exactly as family (1) is, and on the same
       evidence — the saves that FOLLOW a frame setup obey the same rigid `ebx ecx edx esi edi`
       order as the ones that precede it. Measured on both committed `-of+` fixtures, every
       frame-first run in them conforms: `wprologue` emits `52`, `51`, `56`, `56 57`, `53 51`,
       `51 56 57`, `53 51 52 56`; `fnpattern` emits `52`, `56 57`. So Ghidra's `01010...`
       (0x50-0x57, any order) becomes the 10 ordered pairs — strictly tighter than the pattern
       Ghidra already ships and accepts standalone on gcc.

       Each of the six is stated twice, for `89 e5` and for Watcom's `8b ec` encoding.

       STILL NOT COVERED, deliberately: a frame setup followed by ordinary code and NOT preceded
       by recognised filler — `55 89 e5 40` (inc eax), `55 89 e5 e8` (call), both of which occur
       in `wprologue`. Covering those needs a naked `0x5589e5`, which no Ghidra pattern file
       states; it would be an invention, and an unmeasurable one until precision can be checked
       on a binary where every function is known.
       ========================================================================================= -->

